WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.100
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.940
I’m at the office. It's too early.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:03.780
Hey, Nick and Nicole!

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:04.900
Nick: Hi.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:05.400
Hank: Hi.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:05.900
Nicole: Hi.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.580
We’re doing something weird today.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.360
We’re gonna show you in just a second.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:10.370
Hank: Hello!

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:11.370
Denise: Hi, how are you?

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.060
Hank: Good! How are you doing?

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:12.570
Denise: Good.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.040
Hank: This is Superintendent Juneau.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.020
She is running for Congress.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:16.520
Denise: Yup.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.180
Hank: To represent the state of Montana.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.760
You’re talking to my brother right now.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.400
Denise: Oh, hi! How are you?

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.560
Hank: So we do all our shooting down here.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.820
Oh yeah, that looks good.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.280
Denise: Thank you!

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.560
Hank: Yeah
Nick: Go. Thank you.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.720
Hank: Super official.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.620
Hank: This is my audience.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:33.800
Denise: Hi, audience!

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.090
Hank: And also my brother.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:36.990
Denise: Hi, John.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:43.741
Hank: I wanted to talk to you, and I’m so
grateful for you, for doing this, because

00:00:43.741 --> 00:00:48.181
I don’t really want to talk about policy
or about your campaign or what you’re gonna

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:51.580
do for Montana when you’re in Washington,
which I imagine is most of what you talk about, which is good.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:52.370
Denise: Right.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:57.030
Hank: I just want to talk about, like, government,
being an elected official, which you are right now

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:57.560
Denise: Yup.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:02.840
Hank: and, you know, how things have gotten
very partisan, and how that affects you running

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.990
for office, and you as a person also, ‘cause
I think oftentimes we forget that politicians are people.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.580
But before we get into any of that, I’m
gonna give you a pop quiz.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.080
Denise: All right.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.300
Hank: I think you will know the answers to
all of these ones.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.220
Denise: I hope so.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.720
Hank: You are running for?

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.320
Denise: U.S. House of Representatives.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.760
Hank: And that is?

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.920
Denise: A House of Congress.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.119
Hank: So members of the House of Representatives
do what?

00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:22.520
Denise: They make laws.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.880
Hank: And the final question of the pop quiz
is how do you expect to afford the rents in D.C. if you win?

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.320
Denise: That’s gonna be a tough one.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.800
Probably get roommates and have to figure
out who my friends in Congress are going to be.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.580
Hank: You got all the questions right.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:37.080
This is a goat.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.200
Denise: Oh, awesome! Thank you so much.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.580
Hank: Everyone gets a goat!

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.300
Nick: Everyone gets a goat.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.460
Hank: You guys don’t get goats, I’m sorry. I was lying.
Denise: On MSU campus, they actually,

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.340
they've been doing a lot of work registering voters, and they have a vote goat.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.340
Hank: Oh, okay.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.870
Is it a live goat?

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.659
Denise: It is a live goat.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.660
Hank: Like, if you vote, you get to like pet
the goat?

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:55.560
Denise: I don’t know!

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.400
It’s just a vote goat that goes around with
them to draw people over so then you can register them to vote.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.780
Hank: Now, we are on to everyone’s favorite
topic, which is their own self, so we’re

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.420
gonna talk about you.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.009
So you currently hold an elected office.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:10.049
You are the Superintendent of Public Instruction.

00:02:10.049 --> 00:02:10.929
Denise: Correct.

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:14.820
Hank: Which is just like the head of—
Denise: The head, yeah, it’s the head of

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.610
all the schools in Montana, basically.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.360
Every four years, people vote for this position,
and it’s one of our statewide offices per

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.540
our state constitution.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:26.640
Hank: Was your campaign for the statewide
office, was that significantly different than

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.820
what you’re experiencing now
Denise: Yes

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.850
Hank: running for a more high-profile thing?

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:30.840
Denise: Yes, totally.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:35.319
You know, the issues are bigger, there’s
more scrutiny, you know, there’s all, there’s

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:37.630
a larger microscope on you.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:40.690
You also get a bigger megaphone, and so that’s
good, too.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:45.230
Hank: I imagine there’s also tough parts
of that, like not a lot of time scheduled for sleep.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.750
Also, there’s a lot of people who are gonna
be saying nasty things about you.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:48.250
Denise: Sure.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.750
Hank: I’m just curious what the worst part
is, I guess.
Denise: It is that.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.420
I think it’s the negativity in politics
right now.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.440
It’s the divisiveness.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:00.370
Good ideas sometimes don’t get to be discussed
because we draw ideological lines and then

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:04.590
talk about, you know, fear-based sorts of
issues rather than actually talking about

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:05.390
issues that matter.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:10.650
Hank: So is it safe to say that during this
campaign, you kind of came out?

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.650
Denise. Mhm. So I introduced my partner.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.280
You have somebody in your life and they, they
need to be there to support you.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.910
Hank: Did you feel like that was something
that you needed to do for political reasons

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:20.910
as well as personal ones?

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.930
Denise: I, yeah, I think it was a mixture
of both.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:24.760
People expect access.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.300
They want to know who their candidates are,
and I think stepping up to this level from

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:33.240
Superintendent to Congress, it was important
to make sure things were out there because

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.400
of the negativity that can sometimes happen.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.489
And it really hasn’t become an issue, which
is

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:40.230
Hank: Yeah, that’s great
Denise: great!

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.650
I think, particularly when we looked at Montana
and, you know, the stereotypes around our

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:49.760
state and its people, it actually hasn’t
become a political issue.

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:50.489
Hank: Yeah.

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:55.869
Denise: Which I think speaks volumes for our
state and our country and the direction we’re moving.

00:03:55.869 --> 00:03:57.460
Hank: I was on your Twitter yesterday
Denise: Right

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.750
Hank: and it says in your bio, “54th generation
Montanan,” which made me laugh.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.879
I just wanna know, how did you come to that
particular number?

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:10.680
Denise: I just figure it’s a long time,
and, you know, you can try to disprove it.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:17.930
But I started like, American Indian people
have been in here before America was a country,

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:22.259
and it’s important to bring that aspect
of history to this race as well.

00:04:22.259 --> 00:04:26.419
Hank: If you aren’t that tuned in, mostly
what you hear about government is the most dysfunctional things.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.020
Denise: Right.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:30.860
Hank: In America, we have very strong local
government, and I’m worried about the future

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:32.939
in which young people aren’t as interested
in that

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:33.999
Denise: Yup.

00:04:33.999 --> 00:04:38.770
Hank: Because they see government as dysfunctional,
or because they just see it as like an unpleasant

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:39.510
task
Denise: Right

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:40.700
Hank: to try and get into that.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.870
Denise: For every message you get and every
letter you get that says “I hate you”

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:48.320
and “You’re doing the wrong thing” and
“You’re horrible,” you get three or

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.770
four that are really good.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:54.509
Or you run into people where, who say, “My
kid would not have graduated were it not for

00:04:54.509 --> 00:04:56.180
Graduation Matters Montana.”

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:02.220
And yep, you’re gonna always get the haters,
but, you know, the good stuff always outweighs those.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.420
Hank: I believe you, I guess.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:03.940
Denise: Yeah.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:06.260
You should!

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:13.350
Hank: What do you think politicians can do
to make it feel, like, to put the government,

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.600
like the running of our country ahead of winning
the election?

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:17.930
Denise: Be present.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.050
90% of politics is showing up.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:25.419
And you show up where people are gathered
and you listen, and, you know, you put aside

00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:30.539
your partisan hat and you actually listen
to people about, what are your issues?

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:32.360
Why are they challenging?

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.449
And how can we make it better?

00:05:33.449 --> 00:05:38.199
I mean that’s really what the function
of government is, to make things better, and

00:05:38.199 --> 00:05:39.839
that’s why elections matter.

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:44.680
It really does matter who sits in those seats,
that can actually get down and do the business

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:45.680
of their government.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.860
Hank: How terrifying is a debate?

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:50.120
Denise: Oh, it’s scary!
Hank: It seems so scary!

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:50.620
Denise: It is!

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:54.320
I mean, it is something where, I mean, you
kind of have to guess on what you’re going to get asked.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.400
There’s always a curveball, you never know.

00:05:56.409 --> 00:05:58.509
Getting up on that stage, it’s something.

00:05:58.509 --> 00:05:59.509
I mean you
Hank: I can’t imagine

00:05:59.509 --> 00:06:02.409
Denise: are definitely awake.

00:06:02.409 --> 00:06:07.639
Last debate actually had an audience question:
If you’re elected, how will you help LGBTQ

00:06:07.639 --> 00:06:08.819
people in the country?

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:13.160
That sort of was a new question we hadn’t
been asked in previous debates, and so my

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:15.330
answer was, number one, get elected.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:18.560
Hank: That’s not fair!

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:19.200
Denise: It’s very fair.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:26.220
Hank: So, I guess my last question is just
how do you, how do you help Americans believe in government?

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.360
Denise: Well I think, number one, get elected.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:33.300
I mean, I’m not naive about, you know, there’s
a lot that needs to be fixed in government,

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:41.460
and we need to really start moving away from
such divisive politics, but through my life

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:47.280
and through my role as an elected official,
I know that things can happen, and so I do,

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.700
I’m optimistic, and I think most people
who run for office, they want to make sure

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.330
that things get better for the people that
they represent.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.990
You’re stepping up, putting yourself on
the line, you’re opening yourself up for

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:04.080
scrutiny, but you’re also opening yourself
up to have real conversations with people.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:07.879
And when you’re elected, to make a difference,
to make a positive difference for the people

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.720
who elected you. and I think that’s really
powerful.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:10.800
Hank: Well, thank you very much.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:11.800
Denise: Yeah, thanks.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:12.800
Hank: This was fun.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.460
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.159
That’s how I end my videos.

00:07:18.159 --> 00:07:23.640
John, there are so many serious arguments
that need to be had, so many issues that need

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:29.300
to be discussed, that it can sometimes feel
hard to justify just having a conversation

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:35.409
with a person who is being voted on to be
in a public office as if they are a person.

00:07:35.409 --> 00:07:38.580
But I think that it’s really important to
try and do.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:44.839
To imagine these people as people, especially
at this moment when the information silos

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:51.229
and the voter dicommunication strategies are
combining to create the most extreme partisanship

00:07:51.229 --> 00:07:52.819
I've ever seen in my lifetime.

00:07:52.819 --> 00:07:57.139
The result of all of this is that no one ever
talks about the things that work, and so everybody

00:07:57.139 --> 00:07:59.439
thinks that everything’s broken, but it’s
not.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:05.040
Yes, things need to be maintained, and we
all need to work to do that maintainence,

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.520
but that doesn’t mean that it’s never
been worse and that we’re losing and that

00:08:07.529 --> 00:08:09.069
everything is crashing down around us.

00:08:09.069 --> 00:08:15.340
Americans, humans, we have problems right
now, but we are good at taking on problems.

00:08:15.349 --> 00:08:17.309
I obviously cut a lot out of this interview.

00:08:17.309 --> 00:08:19.139
The full interview is like a half an hour
long.

00:08:19.139 --> 00:08:22.400
I would love it if you would go watch the
whole thing.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:25.360
We got deeper into several of the topics from
this video, and then talked about stuff that

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:26.899
isn’t in this video at all.

00:08:26.899 --> 00:08:30.779
I thought it was a fascinating conversation,
and I hope that you will as well.

00:08:30.779 --> 00:08:33.079
You can watch that video here.

00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:34.679
Click there. There's a link in the description.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:39.019
And of course, as a final reminder, educational
videos are exempt from the time limit, so

00:08:39.019 --> 00:08:40.520
stop talking about punishments in the comments.

